from pathlib import Path
p=Path('content/networks/02-ip-routing/ip-bgp.md')
s=p.read_text()
def r(a,b):
 global s
 assert a in s,a[:100]
 s=s.replace(a,b)
r("OSPF assumes one owner who trusts every router and wants the shortest path. The Internet has neither. It's about 75,000 independently run networks, many of them competitors, each with its own commercial interests.","OSPF computes paths within a routing domain using configured costs; that does not make its routers inherently trustworthy. Internet inter-domain routing must also accommodate independent networks, many of them competitors, with different policies.")
r('Each has an **AS number** (ASN), assigned by a regional Internet registry.','Public ASNs are allocated through Internet number registries; private ASNs can be used in appropriate private routing contexts.')
r('- ASNs were originally 16-bit (up to 65,535). 32-bit ASNs (RFC 6793) raised the limit to about 4.3 billion.','- ASN fields expanded from 16 to 32 bits (RFC 6793). Not every encodable value is assignable: 65535 and 4294967295, for example, are reserved.')
r('- Examples: AS15169 is Google, AS13335 Cloudflare, AS3356 Lumen.','- AS numbers in the examples below illustrate routing policy; they are not instructions to announce those routes publicly.')
r("Inside an AS, an IGP (OSPF, IS-IS) handles routing. Between ASes, it's BGP.","An AS can use an IGP such as OSPF or IS-IS internally, alongside internal BGP. eBGP exchanges routes between ASes; designs can also use eBGP inside one organisation.")
r('each route carries the full list of ASes it has passed through, the **AS_PATH**:','an ordinary **AS_PATH** sequence records advertised AS traversal, with repeated entries possible for prepending. It is not cryptographic proof of the actual forwarding path. For example:')
r('if a router sees its own ASN in a received AS_PATH, it rejects the route. No count to infinity.','normal AS-loop checking excludes a received route containing the local ASN. Explicit configuration exceptions exist; this mechanism does not eliminate every possible forwarding loop or policy oscillation.')
r('operators can see, and filter on, exactly which networks a route goes through.','operators can filter or rank the advertised path, subject to the limits of trusting that advertisement.')
r('configured by hand: BGP doesn\'t discover neighbours. The messages are:', 'using configured peer relationships. Base BGP has no OSPF-style multicast Hello discovery, although implementations can automate provisioning or accept dynamic peers under configured rules. Its four base message types are:')
r('"Still here", by default every third of the hold time','Maintain liveness; one third of a nonzero hold time is a suggested maximum interval')
r('After the initial exchange, BGP sends only **changes**. A full IPv4 table is around a million prefixes, so routers that take a "full table" need serious memory.','After the initial permitted route exchange, normal updates are incremental. Route-refresh extensions can request re-advertisements. KEEPALIVE or UPDATE receipt can reset the hold timer; negotiated hold time zero disables this timer and KEEPALIVEs. Memory needs depend on prefixes, paths, attributes and implementation.')
r('so every edge router learns what the others heard from outside.','to distribute BGP reachability internally, subject to selection and export policy.')
r("iBGP doesn't add to the AS_PATH, so it can't use it for loop prevention. Instead, a route learnt over iBGP is never passed to another iBGP peer. That means every iBGP router must peer with every other: a full mesh, n(n−1)/2 sessions. With 100 routers that's 4,950. Large networks use **route reflectors** (RFC 4456), which are allowed to re-advertise iBGP routes to their clients, collapsing the mesh into a hub and spoke.","iBGP does not prepend the local ASN, so the AS_PATH cannot record internal router traversal. In base iBGP, an iBGP-learned route is not re-advertised to another internal peer. A full mesh therefore needs n(n−1)/2 sessions: 4,950 for 100 routers. **Route reflectors** (RFC 4456) relax that restriction: selected client routes can be reflected to clients and non-clients, while non-client routes are reflected to clients. ORIGINATOR_ID and CLUSTER_LIST support reflection-loop detection. Confederations are another scaling approach.")
r('**AS_PATH**: the ASes traversed.','**AS_PATH**: the advertised AS traversal, possibly including prepending.')
r('When a router has several routes for the same prefix, it picks one, roughly in this order (vendors add steps, such as Cisco\'s weight):','Among eligible routes for the same prefix, a common Cisco-style sequence is below. Reachability checks, vendor-specific steps (including Cisco weight before LOCAL_PREF), configuration and multipath settings matter; this is not a universal BGP ordering:')
r('Lowest **origin** type (IGP, then EGP, then incomplete).','Lowest **origin** attribute (IGP, then EGP, then incomplete). These are attribute codes, not proof that the route was learned through an IGP.')
r('Tie-breakers: oldest route, lowest router ID.','Further implementation-dependent tie-breakers, including route age in some eBGP cases and router IDs.')
r('As a Python sort key (simplified: MED is compared across all routes here):','This executable teaching model ranks already eligible routes, assumes equal weight/local-origination status, counts AS_SEQUENCE entries, compares MED across all candidates, and uses input order for complete ties. It deliberately omits the rest of real BGP selection:')
r('best = min(routes, key=rank)','routes = [\n    dict(name="transit", lp=100, path=[64500, 3356, 65010],\n         origin=0, med=0, ebgp=True, igp=10),\n    dict(name="peer", lp=200, path=[64600, 64700, 65010],\n         origin=0, med=0, ebgp=True, igp=10),\n    dict(name="customer", lp=300, path=[65010, 65010, 65010],\n         origin=0, med=0, ebgp=True, igp=10),\n]\nbest = min(routes, key=rank)\nprint(best["name"])  # customer')
r('LOCAL_PREF is checked first, so the customer route wins at 300.','With all eligibility checks passed and earlier vendor-specific preferences equal, LOCAL_PREF decides: the customer route wins at 300.')
r('two networks swap traffic between their own customers for free, typically at an Internet exchange.','two networks exchange agreed traffic; settlement-free peering has no traffic settlement payment between them, but paid peering and infrastructure costs also exist.')
r('The usual policy (the **Gao–Rexford** model) follows the money:','A simplified commercial policy associated with the **Gao–Rexford** model is:')
r('A network never carries traffic between two of its providers for free.','Under these export restrictions a network does not provide transit between its providers. Real contracts and routing policies can differ; convergence results require additional model assumptions, including an acyclic customer-provider hierarchy.')
r('Longest-prefix match sends traffic for each half to its own site.','Where those more-specific routes are accepted and installed, longest-prefix match selects them.')
r('Most networks only accept IPv4 prefixes up to **/24** (and IPv6 up to /48) from outside, to keep the global table in check. So a /24 is the smallest block you can usefully announce.','Public routing policies commonly filter prefixes longer than IPv4 **/24** or IPv6 **/48**, but acceptance depends on the receiving network. More-specific routes can be useful in private or explicitly agreed routing; no announcement length guarantees global propagation.')
r('because longest-prefix match prefers it everywhere it spreads.','because longest-prefix match prefers it where it is accepted and installed alongside the covering route.')
r('for around two hours.','during the interval documented by RIPE RIS from 18:47 to 21:01 UTC on 24 February. YouTube first counter-announced the /24, then two /25s; acceptance varied by network.')
r('Users who clicked past a certificate warning lost an estimated $150,000 or so in cryptocurrency.','Cloudflare documented the redirection and certificate-warning risk; this lesson does not establish a reliable total financial loss.')
r('Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp were down for about six hours.','the backbone disconnection disrupted Facebook services. Cached DNS answers could still exist; DNS failure was a consequence that compounded the underlying network outage.')
r('## RPKI: proving who owns a prefix','## RPKI: authorising route origins')
r('1. The RIRs issue certificates to address holders.','1. Resource certificates form a validation chain rooted in configured trust anchors. They attest resource control within this system, rather than legal ownership.')
r('3. Routers fetch validated ROAs from local validator software and check each route\'s **origin AS** (the last AS in the path).','3. Validator software checks signed objects and supplies validated prefix/origin/max-length records to routers. For the simple AS_SEQUENCE paths here, the origin is the final ASN.')
r('| Valid | A ROA covers it; origin and length match |','| Valid | At least one covering validated record authorises both origin and length |')
r('| Invalid | A ROA covers the prefix but the origin or length is wrong |','| Invalid | Covering validated records exist, but none authorises both origin and length |')
r('Networks deploying ROV **drop Invalid** routes, and many large carriers and clouds now do.','Operators choose how to use validation states; **dropping Invalid** routes is a filtering policy, not an automatic effect of computing ROV.')
r('192.0.2.0/22','192.0.0.0/22')
r('A ROA for 192.0.0.0/22','In a hypothetical prefix-arithmetic example, a ROA for 192.0.0.0/22')
r('is Valid. It doesn\'t stop leaks either, since leaked routes have the genuine origin.','can be Valid when origin and prefix length are authorised. ROV does not validate the intervening path or detect a leak that retains an authorised origin.')
r('**BGPsec** (RFC 8205) signs every hop of the path, but needs every router to do crypto per update and has seen almost no deployment.','**BGPsec** (RFC 8205) provides signed AS-path propagation for participating BGPsec speakers. Its protection depends on cryptographic validation and deployment; it is not a claim that every forwarding router signs every packet.')
r('**ASPA** (autonomous system provider authorisation), being standardised in the IETF, lets an AS publish its providers so others can detect leaks and some forged paths without per-hop signatures.','**ASPA** (autonomous system provider authorisation) publishes authorised provider relationships. The IETF verification draft retrieved for this review is work in progress (August 2026); detection depends on relationship data, direction and validation rules, and is not guaranteed for every forged path.')
r('## Key takeaways','> [!note] Content omitted after review\n> Current AS/route counts, adoption percentages, router performance guarantees and the Route 53 incident\'s financial loss are omitted because this review did not establish reliable dated evidence for them. No live Internet routing changes were performed to validate the examples.\n\n## Key takeaways')
r('whose AS_PATH prevents loops and enables policy.','whose AS_PATH supports AS-loop checks and policy.')
r('because it doesn\'t re-advertise iBGP-learnt routes.','because base iBGP restricts re-advertising internally learned routes; confederations provide another design option.')
r('Best path is chosen by LOCAL_PREF first, then AS_PATH length, origin, MED, eBGP over iBGP and IGP cost: policy, not performance.','LOCAL_PREF and AS_PATH are major selection inputs; exact ordering and tie-breaks depend on implementation and configuration.')
r('RPKI ROV rejects routes with the wrong origin or length, but forged paths and leaks need ASPA, BGPsec or filtering.','RPKI ROV classifies origin authorisation; local policy decides rejection. It does not prove the whole path or eliminate route leaks.')
s += '\n- [RIPE NCC: YouTube hijacking case study](https://www.ripe.net/about-us/news/youtube-hijacking-a-ripe-ncc-ris-case-study/)\n- [Meta: October 2021 outage details](https://engineering.fb.com/2021/10/05/networking-traffic/outage-details/)\n- [Cloudflare: BGP leaks and cryptocurrencies](https://blog.cloudflare.com/bgp-leaks-and-crypto-currencies/)\n- [Cisco: BGP best-path algorithm](https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/support/docs/ip/border-gateway-protocol-bgp/13753-25.html)\n- [IETF: ASPA verification draft](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-sidrops-aspa-verification/)\n- [RFC 9319: RPKI maxLength](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9319)\n'
